WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.700
In his found object sculptures,

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.900
Tobias Eder brings different materials together.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.900
Through his art, he gives them

4
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.630
a longer life than was intended.

5
00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.900
His works become a playful statement

6
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.850
against a throwaway society.

7
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.650
At the same time, they tell charming stories

8
00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.030
through the discarded objects they use.

9
00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:33.500
We visit him in his home,

10
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.400
which is also his studio,

11
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.800
and get an insight into his creative process.

12
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.200
What comes first, the idea or the material?

13
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.600
The idea comes first.

14
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.460
But in the larger concept, it is a trio.

15
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.050
It is a trio of experience,

16
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.500
which has to come first.

17
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.000
Then it settles into you,

18
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.460
consciously or subconsciously.

19
00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.050
That is how an idea can develop.

20
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.650
After that, you find the material

21
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:16.460
to realize the idea.

22
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.250
So the idea comes first.

23
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.470
And the idea only comes through experience.

24
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.050
This will become the body.

25
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.750
I will use this as the base,

26
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.850
the chestnut as the head,

27
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.050
this as the hair,

28
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.550
and this as the hat.

29
00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:39.550
And I can still use the string as an accessory.

30
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:43.000
The idea for the figure came originally

31
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.600
when I was at home in my room.

32
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.950
It was Corona, and we were all completely isolated.

33
00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:55.000
I had this figure from my father,

34
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.300
which we had collected in Denmark.

35
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.030
It stood on a shell,

36
00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.250
right next to the record player.

37
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.030
I had put on a nice record.

38
00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.400
I felt the need to dance

39
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.400
without interacting with anyone.

40
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.030
Then the idea came to me.

41
00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.600
I put three discs underneath it,

42
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.030
and we danced together.

43
00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.450
Again, the experience led to the idea,

44
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.030
the need to dance.

46
00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:24.200
With his figures, he shows

47
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.110
how much beauty can still be found

48
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:28.400
in things that are unused

49
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.110
or no longer wanted.

50
00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.750
Sustainability is definitely

51
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.110
an important factor in my art.

52
00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:39.050
It is one of the defining topics

53
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:42.300
of my generation and future generations.

54
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.110
Every conscious person who walks

55
00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:48.000
through our cities or through nature

56
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.190
sees the rubbish that is simply there.

57
00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:54.300
In this sense, my art is shaped

58
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.000
by collecting my materials from the ground.

59
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.500
Over the years, he has developed

60
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.800
his own techniques for revaluing

61
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:08.790
supposedly worthless things.

62
00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.790
What do I want to transport through art?

63
00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.790
My artwork is a means of communication.

64
00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.790
I don't want to make decorative art.

65
00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.300
The more abstract the communication,

66
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.870
the more powerful the understanding can become.

67
00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.400
If we stay with the vinyl creature,

68
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.870
you buy a record from an artist you like.

69
00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:37.300
You support that artist,

70
00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.870
and then you actively sit down to listen.

71
00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:44.500
Maybe your favorite song is in fourth place,

72
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.870
and you wait for it.

73
00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:50.700
And to really enjoy it,

74
00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.950
you could even let a creature dance with you.
